2186045417] [17592186129387 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129387 #edb/ref 1050 1080.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129387 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "c75874bf-fc95-5847-9b2c-aa230e68a787" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129520 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129520 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161524" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129520 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129520 #edb/ref 1071 "By changing the order of integration, find the missing limits in the following repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-{z}^{2}-1}^{-z}{\\int }_{0}^{2y+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z={\\int }_{-2}^{p}{\\int }_{q}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{r}{\\int }_{0}^{2y+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129520 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "78810200-6809-5a82-a224-a2c79dd59842" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129520 #edb/ref 1074 "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(y)}^{{v}_{4}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{3}(y,z)}^{{u}_{4}(y,z)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,-{z}^{2}-1\\le y\\le -z,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2y+z\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-{z}^{2}-1}^{-z}{\\int }_{0}^{2y+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{2y+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(y)}^{{v}_{4}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{2y+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/59/59e5dae7cb1d621213f3e4ef792623bdfd9a42aef19c75f7ca9d65b3af32eeda.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,-{z}^{2}-1\\le y\\le -z\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we split $D$ into two type I regions.\n\n![](images/9c/9c968af98710367752a9d788b9b4c00d8c04e31d5494f7887ea5d2d635312338.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the left region, written in the form $z=z(y),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $z=\\sqrt{-y-1}\\,$ and $\\,z=1.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the right region, written in the form $z=z(y),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $z=0\\,$ and $\\,z=-y.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le y\\le -1,\\,\\sqrt{-y-1}\\le z\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 0,\\,0\\le z\\le -y\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{-y-1}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{-y}{\\int }_{0}^{2y+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129528 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129528 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162226" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129528 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129528 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c4/c420d45ca9418fd29889d91a49cce4ae80bb71bdfd4e4aa701b495cc8d87335f.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $z,$ then $x,$ and then $y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129528 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "318d728a-5f9a-5d33-846c-76ae520cec78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129528 #edb/ref 1074 "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+y\\}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/8b/8b7cfa72ed3dbfb121039c6e64b843d83489878a9f457fb1386e455e7d9649ec.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is \n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt{y}\\le x\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f4/f49af5da2e4f1f281700e16c4659fabfcd0b15661947ca985d38f208f8563877.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129529 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129529 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162253" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129529 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129529 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{z}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129529 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c00cd44a-d34c-5f6e-aacf-b4ef475837d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129529 #edb/ref 1074 "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt{z}\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {e}^{z/x}\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $y,$ the $y$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/e8/e83c249a6abf9710a201462e16c646a11411dbcdbca46f34e9beb8d2db7a654b.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $xz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\sqrt{z}\\le x\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $z=z(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{z}\\,⟹\\,z={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le {x}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7e/7e684766b85a214d1f1dd7845979052c532e11ecce74d0284b8fc379b5669957.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{z}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}[y]_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{e}^{z/x}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{e}^{z/x}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[x{e}^{z/x}]_{z=0}^{z={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x{e}^{x}-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[(x-1){e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(0-\\frac{1}{2})-(-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129530 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129530 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162527" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129530 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129530 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/02/024df0fe0a965feba2177adfac8bbd658fc90f4775a4707f13eba1ef2cb94702.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $x,$ then $z,$ and then $y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129530 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b08713a3-4d0e-55df-a71c-6cc156f3ac58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129530 #edb/ref 1074 "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{z},\\,\\,1+y-z\\le x\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/8f/8fff232443e6da697862c2e85a9d3abfe0554b80c2fcbfa8c93de42b94a45691.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{z}\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the bottom boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(y),$ is \n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{z}\\,⟹\\,z={y}^{3}\n$$\n\nand the top boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,{y}^{3}\\le z\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/72/726ff420cae25c40f212714141a134fab84633cca7f49bdd268f0668d5248766.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{1}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130215 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130215 #edb/ref 1061 43192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130215 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f86e4250-7fc0-50bb-a0af-7ee8be7d9280" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130215 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130221 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130221 #edb/ref 1061 44531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130221 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "30b66795-9d4b-59ba-8e94-019dc84aa2a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130221 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130223 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130223 #edb/ref 1061 44542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130223 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c026c427-c43c-522a-b3ea-70f779c47b44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130223 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130224 #edb/ref 1061 44544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130224 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "67760aeb-1f74-5ff1-8a5b-c1ab32a14947" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130224 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130458 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to evaluate the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1\\int_{x^2}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1}\\,\\dfrac{e^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\textrm{d}z\\, \\textrm{d}y\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFor this repeated integral, the integration domain is the type I region \n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\:  x^2 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nas illustrated below.\n\n![](images/54/54bdc97100c19657d261f35123eec8a4d2d927f0d92073462ae6e9335aef0921.png)\n\nThe expression '$\\,\\textrm{d}z\\, \\textrm{d}y\\, \\textrm{d}x$' means we should integrate first with respect to $z,$ then with respect to $y,$ and lastly with respect to $x.$ Let's see what happens when we attempt to integrate in this order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot [z]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot proceed any further because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of $\\dfrac{e^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}.$\n\nTo resolve this issue, we first note that the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane is the type I plane region $D$ shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f681a48038d51cdb592b265e7f482aa03fffb2d1235df02b38390083fe73f3a.png)\n\nThis region can also be represented as the type II plane region.\n\n![](images/89/89836e63d7f184624334daef7c50239dc818324f24071118aa41a2a1ca5accb6.png)\n\nUsing set notation, $D$'s representation as a type II plane region is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate this integral by keeping the inner integral the same and swapping the order of integration in the middle and outer integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate this integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}[z]_{0}^{1}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot [x]_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\sqrt{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{y}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =e-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote the following:\n\n- In this lesson, we'll learn how to compute triple integrals by changing the representation of the projection $D$ of $R$ from a type I plane region to type II (and vice-versa).\n\n- It's also possible to change the spatial (i.e., three-dimensional) representation of $R$ from type I to type II, type I to type III, type II to type III, etc. This is slightly more involved, so we'll leave that discussion for a separate lesson.\n\n$$\n%\\eqalign{\n%\\int_0^1\\int_0^1\\int_{{x}}^{1} \\,6z e^{zy^2}\\,\\textrm{d}y\\, \\textrm{d}x\\, \\, \\textrm{d}z &= \\int_0^1\\int_0^1\\int_{{0}}^{y} \\,6z e^{zy^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x\\, \\textrm{d}y\\, \\, \\textrm{d}z\\\n%&= \\int_0^1\\int_0^1 \\,6z e^{zy^2} \\left[x\\right]_{{0}}^{y}\\, \\textrm{d}y\\, \\, \\textrm{d}z\\\n%&= \\int_0^1\\int_0^1 \\,6z ye^{zy^2} \\, \\textrm{d}y\\, \\, \\textrm{d}z\\\n%&=  \\int_0^1 \\, 3 \\left[e^{zy^2}\\right]_0^1 \\,  \\, \\textrm{d}z\\\n%&=  \\int_0^1 \\, 3 \\left(e^z-1\\right)  \\, \\textrm{d}z\\\n%&= 3e-6.}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130458 #edb/ref 1065 7287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130458 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0583a558-7d9d-59a9-b3df-1804f157fe28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130458 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278588 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278588 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278588 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0073c988-ab13-5d35-82dc-f86a2f72a0de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278722 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{\\pi }+2" #edb/ref 1759218