[17592186401329 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401329 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401329 #edb/ref 1071 "By changing the order of integration, find the missing limits in the following repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2y}-1}^{2-y/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{-1}^{p}{\\int }_{0}^{(z+1)^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{1}^{q}{\\int }_{0}^{r}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401329 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f9c68a00-265c-5c7e-8372-6f380b398f1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401329 #edb/ref 1074 "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(z)}^{{v}_{4}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{3}(xy,z)}^{{u}_{4}(y,z)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,\\sqrt{2y}-1\\le z\\le 2-\\frac{y}{2},\\,\\,-2z\\le x\\le y+3\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2y}-1}^{2-y/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(z)}^{{v}_{4}(x)}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4de9d9df51efcec67253a1c7118466f29459ffd5c9136c144d33500ec5557bc.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\sqrt{2y}-1\\le z\\le 2-\\frac{y}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we split $D$ into two type II regions.\n\n![](images/18/18bb3e55f076e022176bb60df27d5665ed2d91d75d618969c04ce192378db922.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the lower region, written in the form $y=y(z),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(z+1)^{2}.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the upper region, written in the form $y=y(z),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=4-2z.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le z\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}(z+1)^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le 4-2z\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{(z+1)^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-2z}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401330 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401330 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401330 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401330 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401330 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401330 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401330 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401330 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186401332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401330 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f079fa33-36ea-54da-8c9a-cfed3ba497cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401331 #edb/ref 1080 "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=2,\\,\\,\\,r={z}^{2}-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401331 #edb/re